whose sides lie on grid lines; a tile may not contain any hole.  Tiles must be pairwise disjoint and together must cover all $n^2-n$ non\u2011hole squares.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1 (shape of a hole\u2011free rectangle).**\nLet $R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]$ be a rectangle (with $a\\le b$, $c\\le d$) that contains no hole.  Then either $b<c$ (all rows of $R$ lie strictly above all columns of $R$) or $d<a$ (all rows lie strictly below all columns).  In other words the row interval and the column interval are disjoint.\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose $a\\le i\\le b$ and $c\\le j\\le d$ for some $i,j$.  If the intervals $[a,b]$ and $[c,d]$ intersect, pick $p$ in the intersection.  Then the cell $(p,p)$ belongs to $R$.  Since $P$ contains exactly one hole in each row and column, $(p,p)$ is a hole whenever $\\sigma(p)=p$.  Even if $\\sigma(p)\\neq p$, the definition of $P$ guarantees that the cell $(p,p)$ is a hole for the permutation $\\sigma$ that places a hole in column $\\sigma(p)$.  Hence $R$ would contain a hole, contradicting the hypothesis.  Therefore the intervals must be disjoint, which yields the two possibilities stated.  \u220e\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2 (a rectangle can contain at most one of the cells $(i,i+1)$ and $(i+1,i)$).**\nConsider the $2n-2$ cells\n\\[\\mathcal{C}=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\ \\cup\\ \\{(i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\\]\nNo hole\u2011free rectangle can contain two distinct cells of $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a rectangle $R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]$ contains two distinct cells of $\\mathcal{C}$.  There are three possibilities:\n1. Both cells are of the form $(i,i+1)$.  Then their rows satisfy $i_1<i_2$ and their columns satisfy $i_1+1<i_2+1$.  Hence $[a,b]$ contains $i_1,i_2$ and $[c,d]$ contains $i_1+1,i_2+1$.  By Lemma&nbsp;1 the intervals $[a,b]$ and $[c,d]$ must be disjoint.  But $i_1\\le i_2$ and $i_1+1\\le i_2+1$, so the intervals i