le in row k. Consequently σ(k)+1 ≥ σ(i)+1, and the cell (i, σ(k)+1) coincides with the hole (i, σ(i)), a contradiction. Hence a rectangle can contain at most one of the cells R_i. Therefore at least n-1 rectangles are needed to cover the n-1 cells R_i. An analogous argument applied to the n-1 cells immediately below each hole (the cells (σ^{-1}(j)+1, j) for j with σ^{-1}(j) < n) shows that at least another n-1 rectangles are needed to cover those cells, unless some rectangle already covers one cell of each type. In any case, the total number of rectangles required is at least n. Thus T_min ≥ n = 2025.\n\n- Combining the two bounds we obtain 2025 ≤ T_min ≤ 4048. The exact value remains undetermined.\n\nThus the solution does not determine the exact minimum; it only gives bounds.\n\nThe problem asks: \"Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nThus the solution is incomplete: it does not determine the exact minimum; it only gives a lower bound of 2025 and an upper bound of 4048. So the answer is not determined. The final answer in the solution is \"2025 ≤ T_min ≤ 4048\" and \"The exact value remains undetermined.\" The final answer is not a specific number; it's a range. The problem expects a specific minimum number. So the answer is wrong/incomplete.\n\nThus Level 1: answer correctness. The answer is not correct because the problem asks for the minimum number, not a range. The solution fails to provide the exact minimum. So answer is WRONG. According to the hierarchical decision tree, for non-optimization problems (this is an optimization problem? Let's check: The problem asks for the minimum number of tiles. That's an optimization problem: we need to find the minimum number of tiles. Indeed, it's a minimization problem. So it's an optimization problem. The solution gives a lower bound and an upper bound, but does not determine the exact m