are of $B$ lies entirely below that hole.\\n\\nHence any admissible tiling uses at least\\n\\\\[ (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 \\\\]\\ntiles.\\n\\n---\\n**Upper bound (construction)**\\n\\nWe now exhibit a tiling that uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles, thereby showing that the lower bound is tight.\\n\\nFor each column $j$:\\n* If $\\\\pi(j)>1$, place a vertical tile covering the cells $(i,j)$ with $1\\\\le i\\\\le \\\\pi(j)-1$.\\n* If $\\\\pi(j)<n$, place a vertical tile covering the cells $(i,j)$ with $\\\\pi(j)+1\\\\le i\\\\le n$.\\n\\nThese tiles are $1\\\\times(\\\\pi(j)-1)$ and $1\\\\times(n-\\\\pi(j))$ rectangles, respectively.  They are disjoint because they occupy different columns, and together they cover every square of column $j$ except the uncovered one $(\\\\pi(j),j)$.  For the unique column where $\\\\pi(j)=1$ only the lower tile is needed, and for the unique column where $\\\\pi(j)=n$ only the upper tile is needed.  Consequently the total number of tiles is\\n\\\\[ (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\\\]\\n\\nThis construction satisfies all requirements: each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square (by definition of $\\\\sigma$), the tiles are axis‑parallel rectangles whose sides lie on grid lines, no two tiles overlap, and every other unit square is covered.\\n\\n---\\n**Conclusion**\\n\\nBoth the lower‑bound argument and the explicit construction give the same value.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[ 2n-2.\\\\]\\nFor $n=2025$ this equals $2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048$.\\n\\nHence the answer is \\\\boxed{4048}.\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
