ists of $n$ squares, one in each row and one in each column.  Hence there is a permutation $\\\\sigma$ of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ such that\\n\\\\[\\nU=\\\\{\\\\,(i,\\\\sigma(i))\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n\\\\,\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nWe refer to $(i,\\\\sigma(i))$ as the *hole* in row $i$.\\n\\n**2. Two auxiliary sets.**\\nDefine\\n\\\\[\\nA=\\\\{\\\\,(i-1,\\\\sigma(i))\\\\mid i=2,\\\\dots,n\\\\,\\\\},\\\\qquad\\nB=\\\\{\\\\,(i+1,\\\\sigma(i))\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\,\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nThus $A$ consists of the squares immediately *above* the holes (except in the first row) and $B$ consists of the squares immediately *below* the holes (except in the last row).  Clearly $|A|=|B|=n-1$.\\n\\n**Lemma 1.** *No tile can contain two distinct squares of $A$ (and similarly no tile can contain two distinct squares of $B$).*\\n\\n*Proof.*  Suppose a tile $T$ contains two different squares $(i_1-1,\\\\sigma(i_1))$ and $(i_2-1,\\\\sigma(i_2))$ from $A$, with $i_1<i_2$.  Because $T$ is a rectangle, its set of rows is an interval containing all rows from $i_1-1$ up to $i_2-1$.  Consequently row $i_1$ (which lies between $i_1-1$ and $i_2-1$) also belongs to $T$.  The column $\\\\sigma(i_1)$ belongs to $T$ as well, since $T$ contains $(i_1-1,\\\\sigma(i_1))$.  Hence $T$ would contain the hole $(i_1,\\\\sigma(i_1))$, contradicting the requirement that holes remain uncovered.  The same argument works for $B$.  ∎\\n\\n**Lemma 2.** *A tile cannot contain a square of $A$ and a square of $B$ simultaneously.*\\n\\n*Proof.*  Assume $T$ contains $(i-1,\\\\sigma(i))\\\\in A$ and $(j+1,\\\\sigma(j))\\\\in B$.  Without loss of generality $i\\\\le j$.  Then the rows of $T$ include the interval $[i-1,\\\\,j+1]$, which certainly contains row $i$; the column $\\\\sigma(i)$ is also present because $(i-1,\\\\sigma(i))\\\\in T$.  Hence $(i,\\\\sigma(i))$, a hole, lies inside $T$, a contradiction.  The case $j<i$ is symmetric.  ∎\\n\\n**3. Lower bound on the number of tiles.**\\nBy Lemma 1 each tile can cover at