(i1)) and (i2-1, sigma(i2)) with i1 < i2. Then tile's rows include from i1-1 to i2-1 inclusive. Row i1 is between i1-1 and i2-1, so tile includes row i1. Column sigma(i1) is included because tile includes (i1-1, sigma(i1)). So tile includes (i1, sigma(i1)) which is a hole. So indeed cannot contain two squares from A. Similarly for B.\n\nLemma 2: Suppose tile contains (i-1, sigma(i)) from A and (j+1, sigma(j)) from B. Without loss i <= j. Then tile rows include i-1 to j+1 inclusive, thus includes row i. Column sigma(i) is included because tile includes (i-1, sigma(i)). So tile includes (i, sigma(i)) hole. So cannot contain both A and B.\n\nThus each tile can contain at most one element from A ∪ B. Since |A| = n-1, |B| = n-1, total 2n-2 squares must be covered, each tile covers at most one of them, so at least 2n-2 tiles needed. This lower bound is correct.\n\nNow the construction: For each column c = sigma(i), they place an upper tile covering squares (1,c) to (i-1,c) if i>1, and a lower tile covering squares (i+1,c) to (n,c) if i<n. These are vertical tiles in each column. They are disjoint because they are in different columns (different c). Within a column, the upper tile covers squares above the hole, lower tile covers squares below the hole. They do not overlap because they are separated by the hole row. So each tile covers exactly the squares in A and B respectively. Since there are n-1 upper tiles (for i>1) and n-1 lower tiles (for i<n), total 2n-2 tiles. This matches lower bound.\n\nThus the solution appears correct, reasoning valid, construction explicit. The solution includes explicit construction details: they specify the tiles as vertical covering specific squares. That's concrete details, Category C. So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow we need to check for any presentation issues: The solution is fairly thorough. The only potential minor gaps: they mention \"The number of upper tiles equals the number of rows i>1, i.e. n-1, and the number of lower tiles equals the