                "reasoning_content": "We have a problem: \"Consider a 2025×2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of different sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nThe solution provided: They define n=2025. They argue uncovered squares form a permutation matrix, i.e., exactly one uncovered square per row and per column, so they correspond to a permutation p. Then they define S as the set of left and right neighbours of each uncovered square (except at boundaries). They compute |S|=2n-2. They prove a lemma: any axis-aligned rectangle that does not intersect U can contain at most one element of S. Then they deduce a lower bound: each tile (rectangle) can contain at most one element of S, so number of tiles T >= |S| = 2n-2. Then they construct a configuration achieving 2n-2 tiles: choose identity permutation (uncovered squares are diagonal). For each i=1..n-1 define a horizontal tile covering row i from column i+1 to n; for each j=1..n-1 define a vertical tile covering column j from row j+1 to n. That's 2n-2 tiles. They argue they are disjoint and cover all non-diagonal cells. They also argue each tile contains exactly one element of S, confirming lower bound is tight. So answer is 2n-2 = 4048.\n\nWe need to evaluate according to the hierarchical decision tree. The problem is not an optimization problem? Actually, it asks for \"minimum number of tiles\". That's an optimization problem (minimum). So we must treat it as an optimization problem. According to the decision tree, for optimization problems we must not check answer correctness at Level 1 (i.e., we cannot claim answer is correct or wrong). Instead we must check for arithmetic errors, constraint violations. The