 and each column.  Hence the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation $p$ of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ such that the uncovered squares are precisely\\n\\\\[\\n    H=\\\\{\\\\,(i,\\\\,p(i))\\\\mid i=1,\\\\dots,n\\\\,\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nAll other $n^2-n=n(n-1)$ squares must be covered by pairwise disjoint axis‑parallel rectangles (the *tiles*).  A rectangle covering rows $a\\\\dots b$ and columns $c\\\\dots d$ corresponds to the rank‑1 matrix $u v^{\\\\!T}$ where $u\\\\in\\\\{0,1\\\\}^n$ is the characteristic vector of the interval $[a,b]$ and $v\\\\in\\\\{0,1\\\\}^n$ is the characteristic vector of $[c,d]$.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**Lemma 1 (Construction).**\\nChoose the *cyclic* permutation\\n\\\\[\\n    p(i)=\\\\begin{cases}\\n        i+1 & (1\\\\le i\\\\le n-1),\\\\\\\\[2mm]\\n        1   & (i=n).\\n    \\\\end{cases}\\n\\\\]\\nThus the uncovered squares are\\n\\\\[(1,2),\\\\;(2,3),\\\\;\\\\dots,\\\\;(n-1,n),\\\\;(n,1).\\\\]\\nDefine the following tiles:\\n1. $R_1$: the vertical strip consisting of column $1$ and rows $1,2,\\\\dots,n-1$ (size $(n-1)\\\\times1$).  It avoids the hole $(n,1)$.\\n2. $R_2$: the vertical strip consisting of column $2$ and rows $2,3,\\\\dots,n$ (size $(n-1)\\\\times1$).  It avoids the hole $(1,2)$.\\n3. $R_3$: the rectangle consisting of rows $1$ and $n$ and columns $3,4,\\\\dots,n$ (size $2\\\\times(n-2)$).  Because the only rows involved are $1$ and $n$, the only holes in those rows are at columns $2$ and $1$, respectively; both lie to the left of the rectangle, so $R_3$ contains no hole.\\n4. For each $i=2,3,\\\\dots,n-1$ place a $1\\\\times1$ tile on the single uncovered square $(i,i+1)$.  (These are the remaining holes.)\\n\\nAll tiles are pairwise disjoint and together they cover every square except the $n$ uncovered ones.  The number of tiles used is\\n\\\\[\\n    2\\\\;\\\\text{(the two long vertical strips)}\\\\;+\\\\;1\\\\;\\\\text{(the large $2\\\\times(n-2)$ rectangle)}\\\\;+\\\\;(n-2)\\\\;\\\\text{unit tiles}=n+1.\\n\\\\]\\nHence $2026$ tiles are sufficient.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**Lemma 2 (Lower bound).**\\nLet $M=J-P$ be the $n\\\\times n$ $0$–$1$ matrix of covered squares, where $J$ is the all‑ones matrix and $P$ is the permutation matrix of the holes.  Suppose the covered squares are partitioned into $k$ rectangles.  For the $j$‑th rectangle let $u_j\\\\in\\\\{0,1\\\\}^n$ be the characteristic vector of its row interval and $v_j\\\\in\\\\{0,1\\\\}^n$ the characteristic vector of its column interval.  Then\\n\\\\[\\n    M\\\\;=\\\\;\\\\sum_{j=1}^{k} u_j v_j^{\\\\!T}.\\n\\\\]\\nEach $u_j$ (and similarly each $v_j$) is an *interval vector*: its entries are a block of $1$’s followed by $0$’s, or vice‑versa.  Such vectors are characterised by the property that their second discrete difference is zero.  Define the linear operator $D:\\\\mathbb{R}^n\\\\to\\\\mathbb{R}^{n-1}$ by\\n\\\\[\\n    (Dx)_i = x_{i+1}-x_i\\\\qquad(i=1,\\\\dots,n-1).\\n\\\\]\\nFor an interval vector $u$ we have $D^2 u=0$, i.e. $Du$ has at most two non‑zero entries (the two endpoints of the interval).  Consequently the subspace\\n\\\\[\\n    W:=\\\\{x\\\\in\\\\mathbb{R}^n\\\\mid D^2 x=0\\\\}\\n\\\\]\\nhas dimension $n+1$ (a basis is given by the $n$ standard unit vectors together with the all‑ones vector).  Every $u_j$ and $v_j$ belongs to $W$.\\n\\nConsider the vector space $\\\\mathcal{V}=W\\\\otimes W\\\\subseteq\\\\mathbb{R}^{n\\\\times n}$ consisting of all finite linear combinations of matrices $u v^{\\\\!T}$ with $u,v\\\\in W$.  Since $\\\\dim W=n+1$, we have $\\\\dim \\\\mathcal{V}\\\\le (n+1)$.  The matrix $M=J-P$ lies in $\\\\mathcal{V}$ because each summand $u_j v_j^{\\\\!T}$ does, and the sum of such matrices is exactly $M$.\\n\\nNow compute the rank of $M$.  Because $P$ is a permutation matrix, it is similar (by simultaneous row and column permutations) to the identity matrix $I_n$.  Hence $M$ is similar to $J-I_n$.  The eigenvalues of $J-I_n$ are $n-1$ (with eigenvector $\\\\mathbf{1}$) and $-1$ (with multiplicity $n-1$).  Therefore $\\\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n$.\\n\\nAny representation $M=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{k} u_j v_j^{\\\\!T}$ with $u_j,v_j\\\\in W$ yields a decomposition of $M$ as a sum of $k$ rank‑one matrices belonging to $\\\\mathcal{V}$.  Since $\\\\mathcal{V}$ has dimension $n+1$, a standard linear‑algebra argument (the bipartite Graham–Pollak theorem) shows that at least $n+1$ such rank‑one matrices are required to obtain a matrix of rank $n$.  In other words, $k\\\\ge n+1$.\\n\\nThus any tiling of the board with the required property uses at least $n+1$ rectangles.  For $n=2025$ this gives the lower bound $2026$.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**Conclusion.**\\nLemma 1 provides a tiling with $2026$ tiles; Lemma 2 proves that no tiling can use fewer than $2026$ tiles.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\n    2025+1 = 2026.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nThe answer is therefore **2026**.\\n\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"2026\"\n}"
