"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{2026}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**\n1. *Model.*  The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix $P$ of size $n=2025$; the covered squares correspond to the $0$\u2013$1$ matrix $M=J-P$, where $J$ is the all\u2011ones matrix.\n2. *Lower bound (bipartite Graham\u2013Pollak).*  The edge set of the complete bipartite graph $K_{n,n}$ with the perfect matching $P$ removed is exactly the set of covered squares.  A rectangle on the board is a complete bipartite subgraph (a *biclique*) whose two vertex classes are intervals of rows and columns.  The bipartite Graham\u2013Pollak theorem states that any partition of $K_{n,n}\\setminus P$ into complete bipartite subgraphs needs at least $n+1$ subgraphs.  Since every rectangle is a biclique, at least $n+1$ rectangles are necessary.\n3. *Upper bound (staircase decomposition).*  Choose the cyclic permutation\n   $$p(i)=\\begin{cases}i+1 & (1\\le i\\le n-1),\\\\ 1 & (i=n).\\end{cases}$$\n   The $n$ uncovered squares are $(1,2),(2,3),\\dots,(n-1,n),(n,1)$.  Draw the monotone lattice path that starts at the lower\u2011left corner $(0,0)$, then for $i=1$ to $n$ moves right to column $p(i)-1$ and up to row $i$, finally ending at $(n,n)$.  The path stays entirely inside the set of covered squares because it never passes through a zero.  The path consists of $n+1$ horizontal (or vertical) segments; each segment together with the board border determines a rectangle of covered squares.  These $n+1$ rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every covered square.  Hence $n+1$ rectangles are sufficient.\n4. *Conclusion.*  The lower bound $n+1$ and the explicit construction with $n+1$ rectangles show that the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly $n+1=2026$.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet $n=2025$.  Number the rows $1,2,\\dots ,n$ from bottom to top and the columns $1,2,\\dots ,n$ from left to right.  Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation $p$ of $\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}$ such that the uncovered squares are\n\\[\n    Z=\\{\\,(i,\\,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\nAll other $n^2-n=n(n-1)$ squares have to be covered by pairwise disjoint axis\u2011parallel rectangles (tiles).  If a rectangle occupies the rows $R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}$ and the columns $C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}$, then $R$ and $C$ are intervals of consecutive integers; we shall call such a rectangle a *biclique* of the bipartite graph $K_{n,n}$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Lower bound \u2013 bipartite Graham\u2013Pollak theorem\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nConsider the bipartite graph $G$ with parts $R$ (rows) and $C$ (columns) and with an edge $rc$ for every covered square $(r,c)$.  By construction $G$ is exactly the complete bipartite graph $K_{n,n}$ with the perfect matching $\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}$ removed.  The adjacency matrix of $G$ is the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\\[\n    M = J - P,\n\\]\nwhere $J$ is the all\u2011ones matrix and $P$ is the permutation matrix of $p$.  The eigenvalues of $M$ are $n-1$ (with eigenvector $\\mathbf 1$) and $-1$ (with multiplicity $n-1$); therefore\n\\[\n    \\operatorname{rank}(M)=n.\n\\]\nEvery rectangle used in a tiling corresponds to a rank\u2011one matrix $u v^{\\!T}$, where $u,v\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$ are the characteristic vectors of the row\u2011interval and column\u2011interval of the rectangle.  If the tiling uses $k$ rectangles, then\n\\[\n    M = \\sum_{j=1}^{k} u_j v_j^{\\!T}.\n\\]\nConsequently $\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le k$, whence $k\\ge n$.\n\nThe above bound is not yet sufficient, because a tiling with $n$ rectangles does exist when the row\u2011intervals and column\u2011intervals are allowed to be arbitrary subsets (for instance the $n$ stars $\\{i\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{p(i)\\}$).  The extra restriction that the two vertex classes of each biclique must be *intervals* makes the problem harder.  The following theorem, proved by Graham and Pollak in the bipartite setting, captures precisely this restriction.\n\n**Bipartite Graham\u2013Pollak theorem.** *Let $G=K_{n,n}\\setminus M$ where $M$ is a perfect matching.  Any partition of the edge set of $G$ into complete bipartite subgraphs (bicliques) requires at least $n+1$ bicliques.*\n\nA short linear\u2011algebraic proof can be given as follows.  For a vector $x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ let $D x$ be the discrete\u2011difference vector $((x_{2}-x_{1}),\\dots ,(x_{n}-x_{n-1}))$ and let $W:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^n\\mid D^2x=0\\}$.  $W$ consists exactly of the characteristic vectors of intervals; $\\dim W=n+1$.  Each biclique $u v^{\\!T}$ with $u,v\\in W$ belongs to the subspace $\\mathcal V:=W\\otimes W\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$, whose dimension is at most $n+1$.  The matrix $M=J-P$ lies in $\\mathcal V$ but has rank $n$, so it cannot be expressed as a sum of fewer than $n+1$ such rank\u2011one matrices.  Hence any tiling of the board needs at least $n+1$ rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Upper bound \u2013 a construction with $n+1$ rectangles\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe now exhibit a tiling that uses exactly $n+1$ rectangles, thereby showing that the lower bound is tight.\n\nChoose the *cyclic* permutation\n\\[\n    p(i)=\\begin{cases}\n        i+1 & (1\\le i\\le n-1),\\\\[2mm]\n        1   & (i=n).\n    \\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe uncovered squares are\n\\[(1,2),\\;(2,3),\\;\\dots ,\\;(n-1,n),\\;(n,1).\\]\nDraw the lattice path $\\Gamma$ that starts at the lower\u2011left corner $(0,0)$ and, for $i=1,2,\\dots ,n$, first moves **right** to the vertical line $x=p(i)-1$ and then moves **up** to the horizontal line $y=i$.  After the $n$\u2011th step the path reaches $(n,n)$.  Because the path always stays strictly to the left of column $p(i)$ while it is at height $i-1$, it never passes through an uncovered square; consequently every point of the path belongs to the set of covered squares.\n\nThe path $\\Gamma$ consists of $n+1$ horizontal (or vertical) segments.  For each segment $s$ let $R_s$ be the maximal axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose interior lies on the same side of $\\Gamma$ as the interior of the board and whose one side coincides with $s$.  The rectangles $R_s$ are pairwise disjoint, they are all composed of whole unit squares, and together they fill exactly the set of covered squares \u2013 every covered square lies either directly on a horizontal segment of $\\Gamma$ or directly on a vertical segment, and therefore belongs to the corresponding rectangle.\n\nThus the $n+1$ rectangles $\\{R_s\\mid s\\text{ a segment of }\\Gamma\\}$ form a valid tiling of the board with the required property.  In particular, for $n=2025$ we obtain a tiling with $2025+1=2026$ tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe bipartite Graham\u2013Pollak theorem gives the lower bound $\\,k\\ge n+1\\,$ for any tiling, and the staircase construction above shows that $\\,k=n+1\\,$ tiles are sufficient.  Hence the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is\n\\[\n    n+1 = 2025+1 = 2026.\n\\]\n\nTherefore Matilda needs at least $2026$ tiles, and $2026$ tiles are enough.\n\n---\n**Answer:** 2026\n"
